{
  "dom_id": "step-q28529",
  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "Which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)$ where $f(x)=\\frac{1}{8}(x+4)(x+2)^{2}?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)",
  "worked_solution": "The equation is already in factored form, so by the zero-product rule, we deduce that it has a single root at $x=-4$ and a double root at $x=-2.$\n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by substituting the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{8}(0+4)(0+2)^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}(4)(2)^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}(4)(4) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the curve\n\n- intercepts the $y$-axis at the point $(0,2),$\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=-4,$ and\n\n- touches the $x$-axis and turns around at the double root $x=-2.$\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is positive, the graph takes an inverted $\\text{S}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14238854/assets/021fddfcb24a74f7ed6ce4007561844375089b81c3aa1d6a42b6da9dc769df90.png)",
